WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.060
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 144, coming to you on July 27th,

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:06.820
2026.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.420
New week, new discoveries, let's see what's happening.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.460
A seed that circled the moon now grows into a living tree in the middle of Manhattan.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.600
Blue origin will conduct hot fire tests of new Glenn upper stages at NASA's Stennis

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.340
Space Center.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.540
The company is rebuilding its Cape Canaveral Launch Complex after earlier infrastructure work.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.860
Stennis offers existing test stands suited for large liquid hydrogen engines.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.540
Engineers will use the data to qualify the stage for flight.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.300
The first new Glenn orbital attempt remains targeted for later this year.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:48.260
From one US company scaling up heavy lift capability, we move to another firm expanding

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.060
its national security reach on a different coast.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.460
Rocket Lab will build a new launch site at Kodiak, Alaska, under a Space Force contract.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:03.660
The facility is designed for up to 18 suborbital missions supporting military testing.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.040
Construction will begin soon and create a second US launch location for the company.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:15.480
The deal expands Rocket Lab's ability to serve national security customers from both coasts.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.000
No orbital launches are currently planned from the site.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:25.360
While companies build new rockets and pads, a simpler story reminds us why people keep looking up.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.780
A tree grown from a seed that flew around the moon on Artemis 1 now stands in Madison Square

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:37.200
Park. The Madison Square Park Conservancy unveiled the sapling during a public event.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.940
The seed traveled aboard Orion before returning to Earth for germination.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.560
Organizers chose a dawn redwood for its historical connection to earlier moon tree projects.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.140
The tree will remain on public display in the park.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.300
From a living reminder of lunar exploration, we turned to how researchers are using

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.640
living animals to read the health of an entire ecosystem.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:06.200
Researchers at Arizona State University modeled how thick cloud layers alter heat flow inside

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.940
sub-neptunes. The clouds trap heat near the surface, changing the temperature gradient that

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:18.380
reaches the rocky core. This feedback can expand or compress the planet's interior over time.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.940
The study used atmospheric circulation models tied to interior structure codes.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.280
Follow-up observations with JWST will test the predicted cloud signatures.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.180
From cloudy worlds close to home, we zoom out to the most extreme objects in the universe

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:41.740
and how they may pair more than once. Gravitational wave detections now suggest some black holes

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:48.120
merge more than once. When two black holes collide, the resulting larger object can later pair

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.900
with another companion. The pattern appears in the mass and spin distributions recorded by

00:02:53.900 --> 00:03:00.420
LIGO and Virgo. Repeating mergers help explain the population of unexpectedly heavy black holes.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.460
Future runs will test how common the second generation events truly are. While detectors listen

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:13.320
for distant collisions, other instruments watch for rapid flashes much closer in our own galaxy.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:23.800
Chinese astronomers found gamma ray pulsations from PSR J0435 plus 3233 using Fermi telescope data.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:30.920
The pulsar spins once every 3.2 milliseconds and lies roughly 3,900 light years away.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:37.020
Its gamma ray beam provides a new probe of the extreme magnetic and rotational environment near

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:43.720
the neutron star. The detection adds to the small sample of millisecond pulsars visible at high energies.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.600
More timing observations will refine its spin-down rate and age estimate.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:55.280
From one precise cosmic clock, we look at tools being built to study entire planets around other stars.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.700
University of Central Florida researchers are developing the photonics-enabled exoplanet spectroscopic system.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:08.480
The instrument aims to improve atmospheric measurements for NASA's planned habitable worlds

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:15.780
observatory. It combines photonic technologies to boost signal-to-noise ratios on faint exoplanet light.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.440
Early lab tests target the wavelength ranges needed to spot potential biosignatures.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.500
The project supports the broader goal of characterizing Earth-like worlds around nearby stars.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:34.360
While future telescopes are prepared, near-term observations track how Earth's own wildlife responds

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:40.580
to changing conditions. Scientists are using the color of a deli penguin guano to track what

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:46.760
the birds eat. Pinker droppings indicate more krill consumption while greener hues point to fish.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:53.680
Satellite images of colony stains help researchers monitor diet changes without disturbing the penguins.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.640
Sea ice conditions strongly influence which prey species remain available.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:03.640
The method gives a broad view of how warming affects the Antarctic food web.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:10.080
From living indicators on Earth, we return to the sky where planets and stars continue their slow dance.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.980
Saturn appears motionless against the stars and Pisces as it reaches a stationary point at 7 pm

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:23.660
Eastern daylight time. The planet sits highest in the pre-don sky, offering the best viewing window

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:29.360
a few hours before sunrise. Its rings remain tilted toward Earth at a favorable angle.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.860
Observers with small telescopes can still spot the planet's largest moon, Titan.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:40.980
The stationary moment marks the transition from westward to eastward motion in Earth's sky.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.300
From a planet that seems to pause, we recall a visitor from the outer solar system

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:49.020
that did not survive its encounter with the Sun.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:58.740
Comet C, over 1999 S4 linear, was discovered in 1999 and disintegrated while approaching the Sun.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.620
Hubble images from July 2000 captured fragments separating as the nucleus crumbled.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:10.560
The comet's unusual brightness fade and sudden disintegration drew wide attention at the time.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.100
Its breakup offered a rare view of how cometary nuclei respond to solar heating.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:20.920
Archival data from the event still inform models of comet fragility.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:27.340
From fragile natural objects, we close with an idea about whether advanced civilizations might

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:33.580
deliberately alter stars. A new pre-print examines whether advanced civilizations might extract

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.480
rotational energy from stars instead of building Dyson spheres.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.700
The author models how such extraction would alter a star's spin rate and observable properties.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:48.540
Mid-infrared waste heat searches would miss this signature entirely.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:54.400
The idea shifts attention toward stars that appear to spin slower than expected for their age.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.420
Follow-up studies will test whether current surveys could already detect the effect.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:03.280
Clouds on sub-neptunes do more than block starlight.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.400
ASU models show they trap heat near the surface, shifting the temperature profile that reaches

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:13.360
the planet's rocky core and changing its overall size and density.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:20.000
JWST transmission spectra can now test these predictions by measuring cloud top temperatures

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.880
and compositions on a growing sample of worlds.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:29.100
If the feedback loop holds, many sub-neptunes may hide larger rocky interiors than current

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:35.280
mass radius diagrams suggest. The next step is comparing the new interior models against the

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:41.700
first multi-wave-length phase curves of cloudy sub-neptunes. What fraction of the sub-neptune

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:47.960
population actually follows this cloud-regulated pathway? If every black hole merger released

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:52.660
energy equal to several suns converted entirely into gravitational waves,

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:58.280
the total output from one event would still be invisible to ordinary telescopes yet

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:03.860
shake space-time across billions of light years. When two black holes spiral together,

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:08.740
the final object can carry enough mass and spin to capture a new partner later,

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:14.800
creating a second-generation merger whose heavier mass and distinctive spin signature stand out

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:20.820
in the data. LIGO and Virgo have already recorded candidates whose masses exceed what single

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.740
collapse stars are expected to produce, pointing to this repeated pairing process.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:32.220
The rate of such events depends on how often black holes remain bound inside dense star clusters,

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:38.240
rather than being ejected after the first collision. Current catalogs contain only a handful

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:44.160
of these candidates, but the number is rising with each observing run. We still do not know what

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.920
fraction of all mergers follow this repeat pathway or how that fraction changes with cosmic time.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:56.080
New instruments and fresh data keep rewriting what we thought we knew about both nearby worlds

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.640
and distant extremes. Keep an eye on the next round of gravitational wave catalog updates.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:05.920
Which could clarify how often black holes merge more than once.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:11.800
That covers today's space and science news. Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.280
found it interesting. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, see you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch

